course: dictionary
lesson: 13670
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one to use. Look at the words around the word and ask what would make sense. «Grandma made an infusion of mint leaves and hot water for our tea.» Here an infusion is a drink, because the leaves and hot water are clues.
explain: Now read this: «The nurse checked the infusion running into his arm through a thin tube.» The tube and the arm show that infusion means medicine or fluid going slowly into a vein. It is a drip, not a drink.
explain: The third meaning is about change. «The visiting artist brought an infusion of fresh ideas to our class.» Here an infusion is something new that brings energy. Swap in your meaning and check: does the sentence still make sense? If it does, you have found the right one.
fact: An infusion is a drink made by soaking leaves, flowers or fruit in hot water, like herbal tea.
fact: In medicine, an infusion is fluid or medicine put slowly into a vein through a tube, also called a drip.
fact: «An infusion of new ideas» means something new that brings energy or change.
fact: A mint or camomile infusion is like tea made without tea leaves.
fact: Infusion is a noun, and it can be countable (infusions) or uncountable (some infusion).
exercise: «The camomile infusion smelled sweet and calming.» What is an infusion in this sentence? | A long, loud noise | *A drink made by soaking flowers in hot water | A type of tree | A tin of paint
exercise: «The nurse started an infusion of fluid through a tube into his arm.» What does infusion mean in this sentence? | *Medicine or fluid going slowly into a vein | A sort of bandage | A cut on the arm | Cleaning the tube with soap
exercise: «The club got an infusion of new money, so it could buy new kit.» What does an infusion of money suggest? | A loss of money | A bill to pay | A bank that closed | *A boost that brings something new
test: «Her class got an infusion of fresh ideas from the visiting artist.» What does infusion mean here? | A type of fruit | A broken window | *Something new that brings energy or change | Something old and boring
test: «The patient's infusion ran slowly through the tube.» Which word in this sentence is a clue that infusion is medical? | the | slowly | *patient's | ran
exam: «Mum says the herbal infusion will help her sleep.» Which meaning of infusion fits best here? | A type of cake | *A drink made from herbs soaked in hot water | Medicine put slowly into a vein | Something new that brings change
